WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.672 --> 00:00:11.591
This is a painting called "Palisades."

00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:14.761
It shows what once used to live
up along the Hudson River,

00:00:14.803 --> 00:00:17.013
north of New York City.

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:21.226
I actually built it to help fund
a new park in Guatemala.

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:24.688
If you and I were to take a walk
through that site right now,

00:00:24.729 --> 00:00:27.148
we would see some of these
plants and animals,

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.317
but a lot of them would be missing.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:31.820
And to be honest, we wouldn't notice.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.949
Gradual change is really hard
to notice over time.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:43.248
My artistic practice is an investigation
into humanity's relationship with nature,

00:00:43.248 --> 00:00:46.543
both what we have,
but also what we've lost.

00:00:46.918 --> 00:00:50.422
A painting like this one
called "Understory"

00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:52.716
begins with a rubric and a database,

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:54.175
not with drawings.

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:56.261
The rubric is the rule of the painting.

00:00:56.261 --> 00:00:59.264
For this one it is these are new species,

00:00:59.264 --> 00:01:02.434
and the database is built out
of a sampling of plants

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:04.603
and animals that fit that rubric.

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.981
It took me about four months of research,

00:01:07.981 --> 00:01:10.775
probably two or three months of drawing

00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:14.195
and another six months of painting
to complete "Understory."

00:01:14.529 --> 00:01:19.534
Most of my complicated, complex paintings
take roughly a year on average.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.914
These works are my form of activism.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:28.460
When I want to research a bird,
I go to a natural history museum,

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:30.503
and this is exactly what I see.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:34.799
It is a box of specimens,
or also called study skins.

00:01:35.091 --> 00:01:38.345
This happens to be a bird
called paradise parrots.

00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:40.305
They used to live in Australia.

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:41.848
They are now extinct.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.935
Specimens have an amazing value.

00:01:44.976 --> 00:01:46.311
They are ...

00:01:47.562 --> 00:01:48.730
They are two things.

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:53.109
One, they are a genetic library of traits.

00:01:53.151 --> 00:01:57.113
And at the same time,
they carry within their feathers

00:01:57.155 --> 00:01:59.491
and their skin and their scales

00:01:59.532 --> 00:02:03.912
a lot of environmental
data that we can use.

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:07.707
When I check out the specimen,
I do drawings of it,

00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:11.920
I do color studies of it,
I do measurements,

00:02:11.962 --> 00:02:15.173
I take my own measurements
and photographs.

00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:20.637
And I do all this work because I really
want to be able to depict them accurately,

00:02:20.679 --> 00:02:23.223
and I want them to be remembered correctly

00:02:23.264 --> 00:02:27.185
and as if they are living,
not just a dried skin.

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:29.980
This painting is called "Canopy,"

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.734
and it is an exploration of species
that have just been discovered.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:37.862
Every single living thing you see in it,

00:02:37.904 --> 00:02:40.907
every little moss, every insect,

00:02:40.907 --> 00:02:44.577
each and every thing is new
to Western science.

00:02:44.619 --> 00:02:50.583
I say new to Western science
because it has certainly not just evolved,

00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:53.586
and it is not new to the people
who live with it.

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:55.922
They probably have their own name for it.

00:02:55.964 --> 00:02:58.508
But it is new to Western science.

00:02:59.426 --> 00:03:03.304
That newness can carry some risk.

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:07.976
There are collectors who love
to have a new species.

00:03:08.018 --> 00:03:09.602
I call this painting "Trade"

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:12.814
because it is about black market
trade and wildlife.

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:15.900
Each of these things
is taken out of nature

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.446
and sold somewhere,
either legally or not legally.

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:24.159
Both novelty and rarity
have this awful way

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.995
of driving prices straight up.

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:31.458
There are plenty of regulations
about the sale of wildlife.

00:03:31.499 --> 00:03:35.587
There's lots legal protections in place,

00:03:35.628 --> 00:03:39.049
but they just are inefficient

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:44.095
because there is such a drive
for wildlife purchase.

00:03:44.512 --> 00:03:47.265
Imagine all of the wild places
there are in the world,

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:49.809
the million acres,

00:03:49.851 --> 00:03:51.853
all of the markets in the world,

00:03:51.895 --> 00:03:54.773
all of the dark web.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.110
There are just never enough boots
on the ground to save wildlife.

00:03:59.861 --> 00:04:03.573
It's a big drain on all of nature.

00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:09.537
Humanity has a way of taking
what we want and what we need,

00:04:09.537 --> 00:04:14.542
but we have never really learned
or gotten good at putting it back.

00:04:14.542 --> 00:04:16.920
We're like kids or toddlers, maybe.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.215
This painting called "Back"

00:04:20.256 --> 00:04:24.010
is about our hope
that nature is resilient.

00:04:24.511 --> 00:04:27.806
Everything in this painting
falls into one of two categories:

00:04:27.847 --> 00:04:31.059
it was either thought extinct

00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:35.772
and then re-found by pure luck
or very hard work,

00:04:35.814 --> 00:04:39.484
or it was on the very brink of extinction

00:04:39.526 --> 00:04:44.447
and was hauled back with some
sometimes pretty extensive interventions.

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:48.493
So on the upper right,
there's a Mauritius kestrel.

00:04:48.535 --> 00:04:49.702
It's a white bird.

00:04:49.744 --> 00:04:53.039
It went down to actually
four individual birds.

00:04:53.039 --> 00:04:54.791
It took an amazing amount of effort

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:57.669
on the part of a handful of people really

00:04:57.669 --> 00:04:59.838
to get it to breed again.

00:05:00.338 --> 00:05:02.173
These were not so lucky.

00:05:02.215 --> 00:05:04.676
Everything in this painting is extinct.

00:05:04.676 --> 00:05:09.222
Every grass, every egg
represents a bird that's extinct.

00:05:09.264 --> 00:05:11.766
I don't mean that they're extinct
just here or there.

00:05:11.808 --> 00:05:13.893
They're really gone from the world.

00:05:14.352 --> 00:05:17.897
You may be able to tell that I studied
the Dutch still life masters.

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:19.899
That was very much on purpose.

00:05:20.525 --> 00:05:23.153
And I did it because some
of their paintings

00:05:23.194 --> 00:05:26.447
are between 400 and 500 years old now.

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:28.283
Why are they still here?

00:05:28.283 --> 00:05:29.951
They're really beautiful.

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:31.953
That's a great kind of insurance.

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:36.666
And they also were made very, very well.

00:05:36.708 --> 00:05:39.627
Just the materialism is really strong.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.173
So I have borrowed
a bunch of their techniques,

00:05:43.173 --> 00:05:44.883
most of their materials.

00:05:45.216 --> 00:05:48.511
And I marry that with modern science

00:05:48.511 --> 00:05:51.890
so that I can share
with future generations

00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:55.310
some of our understanding
of what's happening

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.437
to our biodiversity right now.

00:05:57.437 --> 00:06:02.901
Like what's struggling,
what's thriving, what's doomed.

00:06:03.776 --> 00:06:07.572
The paintings are kind of like
an environmental snapshot,

00:06:07.614 --> 00:06:10.283
a picture in time.

00:06:10.283 --> 00:06:13.494
Or maybe better,
like a message in a bottle.

00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:17.415
With luck, these paintings
can talk to the future.

00:06:17.749 --> 00:06:20.877
This is a detail from "Gone."

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:24.422
On your left, there's a bird
that I showed you as a specimen,

00:06:24.464 --> 00:06:27.175
the paradise parrot from Australia, gone.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.470
In the middle is a passenger
pigeon, which is extinct,

00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:33.514
despite the fact that there were
millions upon millions of them.

00:06:33.514 --> 00:06:36.893
They used to darken the skies
of this part of America, the Midwest.

00:06:37.268 --> 00:06:41.481
The yellow-headed bird on the right
is a Carolina parakeet

00:06:41.481 --> 00:06:46.611
that used to raucously fill the skies
of the southeast of the US.

00:06:46.903 --> 00:06:50.114
This was the first painting
I did of extinct species.

00:06:50.406 --> 00:06:52.408
So it's kind of "Gone 1."

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:58.539
But I really, really don't want to have
to paint "Gone" 2, 3, 4 or 5.

00:06:58.873 --> 00:07:04.254
When I started college, I thought we were
in the middle of an environmental crisis,

00:07:04.295 --> 00:07:05.672
and we were.

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:08.508
Audubon thought we were
in the middle of a crisis,

00:07:08.549 --> 00:07:11.135
an environmental crisis, in the 1840s.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:16.933
I don't think either he or I could
have possibly imagined

00:07:16.975 --> 00:07:21.104
the amount of cumulative
destruction of nature

00:07:21.104 --> 00:07:23.022
we would see within our lifetime,

00:07:23.064 --> 00:07:25.316
within each of our lifetimes.

00:07:25.316 --> 00:07:29.195
The depth of our disruption of nature
is so extensive now

00:07:29.237 --> 00:07:31.030
and so global,

00:07:31.072 --> 00:07:33.241
that we are, of course,
affecting the systems

00:07:33.283 --> 00:07:35.535
that govern the water,
the weather, the soil,

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:38.079
the oceans, the climate, all of them.

00:07:38.913 --> 00:07:42.542
These are the very systems
that we rely on for our survival

00:07:42.542 --> 00:07:45.503
and that nature relies on
for its survival.

00:07:45.878 --> 00:07:49.173
I think of my paintings
as alarm clocks in a way.

00:07:49.215 --> 00:07:52.218
They are reminders of what's at stake.

00:07:52.218 --> 00:07:55.596
The only real problem is we keep
pushing the snooze button.

00:07:55.638 --> 00:07:59.642
I plan to keep documenting, in paint,

00:07:59.684 --> 00:08:01.394
biodiversity that's at risk,

00:08:01.436 --> 00:08:03.563
making a record of it

00:08:03.604 --> 00:08:06.691
and advocating for biodiversity as I can.

00:08:06.733 --> 00:08:09.861
But what's probably more important
is what is the next message

00:08:09.861 --> 00:08:13.614
that gets carried through
these paintings into the future?

00:08:15.533 --> 00:08:19.620
It's humanity collectively
that will decide that message.

00:08:20.163 --> 00:08:21.873
I've thought it could be:

00:08:21.873 --> 00:08:24.625
well, too bad, these are the ghosts,

00:08:25.501 --> 00:08:27.337
these are what we lost.

00:08:27.337 --> 00:08:30.840
But it would be much better
if that message was,

00:08:30.882 --> 00:08:34.052
this was the moment
we started to take action

00:08:34.093 --> 00:08:38.639
where we really tried
to save life on this planet,

00:08:38.681 --> 00:08:40.141
including our own.

00:08:40.808 --> 00:08:41.976
Thank you.

00:08:42.018 --> 00:08:44.729
(Applause)

00:08:44.771 --> 00:08:45.938
Thank you.

